# Round the training forward the way the rollout engine does.
#
# Apply on top of verl-ue8m0-fp8-scale-grid.patch:
#   cd /app/verl && git apply verl-ue8m0-fp8-scale-grid.patch
#                && git apply verl-qat-fp8-match-rollout.patch
#
# Enable with:
#   actor_rollout_ref.actor.megatron.qat.enable=True
#   actor_rollout_ref.actor.megatron.qat.mode=fp8_blockwise
#   VERL_QAT_KV_FAKE_QUANT=1
#   VERL_QAT_EXTRA_IGNORE='*experts*'
#
#
# What this is for
# ----------------
# rollout_corr/kl is the gap between the log-probs vLLM produced while
# generating and the ones Megatron recomputes for the same tokens. Once the
# ue8m0 fix makes the weights bit-identical, everything left is arithmetic:
# vLLM rounds its operands to FP8 and Megatron does not. Rounding the same way
# during training makes old_log_probs describe the policy that actually
# generated the data, which is what old_log_probs is supposed to be.
#
# This is not the usual reason to do QAT. Nothing here is about making the
# model tolerate low precision later; it is about the training and rollout
# forwards agreeing now.
#
#
# What it rounds, and why exactly this
# ------------------------------------
# Taken from what vLLM actually runs on ROCm/gfx950, not from what a checkpoint
# declares:
#
#   linear activations  E4M3, one plain fp32 scale per 128 values along the
#                       reduction axis. QuantFP8 is constructed with
#                       use_ue8m0=False outright, and the DeepGEMM path that
#                       would round scales to powers of two needs Hopper.
#   linear weights      E4M3, one scale per 128x128 tile, snapped to a power of
#                       two. The checkpoint's grid is ue8m0 and the weight sync
#                       keeps it there, so amax/448 would train against weights
#                       the rollout never holds.
#   KV latent           the first 448 of 512 channels in E4M3 with a power-of-two
#                       scale per 64, the trailing RoPE 64 left alone --
#                       DeepSeek-V4's fp8_ds_mla cache layout. Quantized after
#                       the KV layernorm and before RoPE, matching the order in
#                       vLLM's compress kernel.
#
# The two operands need opposite answers on scale rounding, which is the part
# worth double-checking against the engine rather than assuming.
#
# FP8 KV is not optional on ROCm: DeepSeek-V4's attention asserts on any
# kv_cache_dtype that does not start with fp8, so the rollout cannot stop
# rounding and training has to start.
#
# Gradients use a straight-through estimator.
#
#
# Evidence: where the error goes, layer by layer
# ----------------------------------------------
# Cross-engine relL2 at layer 0, weights bit-identical in all four:
#
#                             base      +QAT    +QAT+KV   +QAT-MoE+KV
#   wq_b input            1.641e-02  2.07e-03  2.07e-03     2.07e-03
#   wq_b output           2.593e-02  7.72e-03  7.72e-03     7.72e-03
#   wo_b output           2.955e-02  2.66e-02  1.60e-02     1.60e-02
#   ffn output            2.120e-02  2.35e-02  2.05e-02     1.85e-02
#   LAYEROUT              2.055e-02  2.25e-02  1.95e-02     1.76e-02
#
# QAT takes 70% off the attention projections. The error then more than triples
# across the attention operator, and rounding the KV latent halves what that
# step adds.
#
# The MoE experts are excluded because measuring said so: fake-quantizing them
# leaves LAYEROUT at 2.25e-02, worse than the 2.06e-02 of not touching them at
# all. Not for the reason first guessed -- Megatron keeps each expert as its own
# 2D weight0..weightN and ModelOpt quantizes them one at a time, so the 128x128
# tiling is already expert-aligned. Excluding only the intermediate between the
# two expert GEMMs, which vLLM's fused kernel leaves in BF16, recovers part of
# it (1.80e-02) but not all, so something else about the MoE path is still
# mismodelled and excluding the experts outright remains the best measured
# option.
#
#
# Evidence: end to end
# --------------------
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash-Base-7L, GRPO, 8192-token responses, bf16 training with the
# ue8m0 fix on both arms, interleaved, n=10 each, exact permutation test.
#
#                        on          off        delta        p
#   rollout_corr/kl      0.003989    0.004612   -13.5%   0.00002
#   probs_diff_mean      0.009181    0.009902    -7.3%   0.00063
#   time_per_step         144.0 s     146.1 s    -1.5%   0.00287
#
#   kl per run, on:  0.003755 0.003832 0.003834 0.003891 0.003923
#                    0.004039 0.004046 0.004078 0.004119 0.004369
#   kl per run, off: 0.004178 0.004408 0.004487 0.004527 0.004560
#                    0.004580 0.004810 0.004838 0.004845 0.004883
#
# Nine of the ten runs with it beat every run without it, and it is not slower:
# at 8192 tokens the rollout dominates the step, so the extra arithmetic in the
# training forward does not show up. At 512 tokens it does -- 425 s against
# 150 s -- so this is cheap only at production response lengths.
#
# Rounding the KV latent on its own is worth about -0.9% (5 steps, one run),
# so nearly all of the effect is the linear operands; the KV rounding matters
# because it is what lets the layerwise error stop growing across attention.
#
#
# Training health, with gradients
# -------------------------------
# Five steps per run with a synthetic reward that gives GRPO groups variance,
# so the advantages do not collapse and the weights actually move:
#
#   run        steps   kl mean    grad_norm      entropy
#   base_a         5   0.004544   0.175-0.234     2.1515
#   base_b         5   0.004652   0.186-0.239     2.1499
#   match_a        5   0.004055   0.183-0.245     2.2141
#   match_b        5   0.004128   0.178-0.213     2.2235
#
# Pooled over the 20 steps that is -11.0%, close to the -13.5% the single-step
# A/B measured, so the improvement survives the weights being updated. Gradient
# norms overlap between the arms, which is the thing that would have to break
# first if the straight-through estimator were causing trouble. Entropy sits
# about 0.07 higher with fake quantization on; two runs per arm is not enough to
# say whether that is real.
#
# Limits worth knowing
# --------------------
# Every run in the n=10 A/B had zero gradient. The 7L debug model scores -1 on
# every sample, so GRPO groups have no variance and the advantages collapse.
# Those runs measure forward-pass agreement, which is exactly what
# rollout_corr/kl is, but they say nothing about what fake quantization does to
# training. A separate 5-step run with a synthetic reward was used to check that
# gradients stay well-behaved; see the note below.
#
# All of it is on the 7L debug model. The production 43L model accumulates
# through more layers and started at a higher KL, so the size of the effect
# there is unmeasured.
#
# The straight-through estimator is an approximation: the forward is rounded,
# the backward is not. Over a long run that is a real difference from plain
# bf16 training, and nothing here measures its effect on final model quality.
diff -ruN base/verl/utils/megatron/kv_fake_quant.py new/verl/utils/megatron/kv_fake_quant.py
--- a/verl/utils/megatron/kv_fake_quant.py	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ b/verl/utils/megatron/kv_fake_quant.py	2026-09-15 19:04:05.449454596 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,133 @@
